            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's entry perfectly aligns with the explicit scriptural interpretations provided in the text, synthesizing the well-established context-dependent meanings of 'seed' (Word of God, children, Messiah) under a single unifying biblical principle derived from Genesis 1: 'a generative word or life planted to reproduce after its kind.' This provides a superior, cohesive explanation for why the exact same symbol applies both to a spoken message (Luke 8:11) and to human offspring or a covenant people (Matthew 13:38, Galatians 3:16), seamlessly linking the biological law of reproduction to spiritual realities. Every specific assertion in the glossary entry is explicitly backed by Jesus' own parabolic keys and apostolic interpretation.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A generative word or life planted to reproduce after its kind, manifesting contextually as the Word of God, a people/children, or the Messiah.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"In the sower it is God's word\",\n    \"In the wheat and tares it is children\",\n    \"The promised Seed is Messiah\",\n    \"One planted person may grow into a house, people, or nation-tree\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The derivation of 'leaven' purely as corrupt doctrine, while textually supported by Matthew 16, contradicts the common tradition reading the parable of the leaven as the positive spread of the kingdom.\",\n    \"The identification of the 'treasure' as the covenant people hidden in the field (the world) challenges the traditional pietistic reading of salvation or Christ being the treasure that the believer buys.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"'Seed' does not possess a single unifying symbolic definition (a generative word or life); rather, it is a versatile agricultural metaphor used disparately for spoken messages (Luke 8), biological descendants (Gen 12), and small beginnings (Matt 13:31), which share only a loose analogical connection based on farming rather than a strict typological unity.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"A flexible agricultural metaphor with disparate, context-dependent meanings lacking a unified ontological definition.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter denies that the various uses of 'seed' share a singular conceptual definition ('generative word or life planted to reproduce after its kind'), treating them instead as disconnected, purely context-dependent metaphors.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"The parable of the sower explicitly defines seed as a spoken message.\",\n    \"The promises to Abraham define seed as biological and spiritual descendants.\",\n    \"The mustard seed emphasizes small size turning into large size, not necessarily reproduction 'after its kind'.\",\n    \"Linguistically, the word for seed has distinct dictionary definitions depending on context, making a single unified synthesis unnecessary.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Unifies the biological law of Genesis 1:11-12 with the spiritual applications in the parables.\",\n    \"Explains why the same symbol can represent both the Word of God and the children of the Kingdom without being contradictory: the Word planted in the heart reproduces the life of the Kingdom in the person.\",\n    \"Explains the progression from a single planted seed (like Abraham in Isaiah 51:2) to a great tree (a nation or people).\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Aligns with standard lexical approaches that divide word meanings strictly by immediate context rather than seeking a unified master symbol across the canon.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Genesis 1:11-12\",\n    \"Matthew 13:19\",